  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/245a7b9af068b057e16a0c84971f5bb356a9fc56374cb3c17c4896d7a9b815ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+b,$ find $\\lambda .$",
  "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $b.$ So, we begin with the vector $b,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/9b99ad62bfedf6945020d345ef0bbc3531c58af1088698a78daf482cd4c14fe1.png)\n\nNote that in $\u25b3OBC,$ we have:\n\n- $BC=4|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=4a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =b+4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+b=b+4a.$\n\nSince $a\u2226b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4\n$$",
